      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "This problem primarily tests the knowledge of the slope of a line, which is derived from ancient Chinese mathematical work \"The Nine Chapters on the Mathematical Art.\" When solving such problems, it is crucial to first determine the slope of the line, then use the property of perpendicular lines: the product of their slopes is $-1$, which is derived from $k\\_1 \\cdot k\\_2 = -1$. Following these steps, we can find the slope of the perpendicular line.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $2x + 3y = 6$, we first convert it to the slope-intercept form $y = kx + b$, where $k$ represents the slope. Hence, we have:\n\\begin{align*}\n2x + 3y &= 6 \\\\\n3y &= -2x + 6 \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2\n\\end{align*}\nFrom this, we can see that the slope $k$ of the line is $-\\frac{2}{3}$.\n\nGiven that we need to find the slope of the line that is perpendicular to this line, we use the property of perpendicular lines stating that the product of their slopes needs to be $-1$:\n$$-\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot k = -1$$\nSolving for $k$ gives us:\n$$k = \\frac{3}{2}$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line $2x + 3y = 6$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}$.",
