      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "First, we express the equation $2x + 3y = 6$ in the slope-intercept form $y = mx + b$, where $m$ is the slope.\n\nStarting with $2x + 3y = 6$, we isolate $y$:\n\\begin{align*}\n3y &= -2x + 6 \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2\n\\end{align*}\nFrom this, we can see that the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{2}{3}$.\n\nThe slope of a line that is perpendicular to another is the negative reciprocal of the original slope. Thus, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to $y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2$ is $-\\frac{3}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line $2x + 3y = 6$ is $\\boxed{-\\frac{3}{2}}$.",
